          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Definition"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            into a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
          %p
            Typically one is interested in the case where the category is equipped with extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structure"} higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#ExtraStructure"} further below
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            is canonically a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
            is canonically a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig"} rig
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            is canonically a plain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %p
            (Combined with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            is often known as a form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            in degree 0, see for instance at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+permutative+category"} K-theory of a permutative category
          %p
            In this sense decategorification is a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+inverse"}> left inverse
            ” to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"}> vertical
            ) “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"}> categorification
            ” (see there for more), namely to the process of asking for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
            analogous to given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theoretic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
          %p
            Crucially, though, decategorification is a systematic process (in fact a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Definition"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of this functor involves making choices: There are in general several categorical structures which have the same decategorification. For instance, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
            (with its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            (with its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig"} rig
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            (see further examples
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Examples"} below
          %p
            More generally, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            there are higher sequences of “higher decategorification” functors which incrementally discard non-invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            by remaining invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
            up to some degree (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DefinitionForHigherCategories"} below
          %p
            In particular, in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+groupoids"} higher groupoids
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-groupoids
            = succeed "-theory," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C0%29-category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,0)
                \-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} truncation
            and the tower of decategorifications/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truncation"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-truncations
            is known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+tower"} Postnikov tower
          %h2#Definition Definitions
          %h3#DefinitionForCategories For categories
          %p
            Given an (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+small+category"} essentially
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(C)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            This construction extends to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
          #eq:PlainDecategorification.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "right center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mi K
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi Cat
                      %mtd
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mtd
                        %mi Set
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %mtd
                        %mo ↦
                      %mtd
                        %mi Obj
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi iso
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{rcl}
                  \\mathllap{K \;\colon\; }
                  Cat &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Set
                  \\\
                  \\mathcal{C} &amp;\mapsto&amp; Obj(\mathcal{C})_{/iso}
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            from the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"}> 2-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+small+categories"} essentially
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            to the category (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+discrete+2-category"} locally discrete 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
          %p
            Notice that we may think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-categories"} 0-categories
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PlainDecategorification"} (1)
            may equivalently be thought of as being of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mn 1
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mn 0
                  %mi Cat
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K \colon 1Cat \longrightarrow 0Cat
                  \\,,
          %p
            which makes manifest that and how decategorification indeed
            = succeed "." do
              %em decreases categorical degree
          %p
            For generalization of decategorification to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#DefinitionForHigherCategories"}> below
            ) it is useful to make explicit that the decategorification 2-functor
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PlainDecategorification"} (1)
            factors as
          #eq:PlainDecategorificationFactoredThroughCore.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Cat
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Core
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mn 0
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K
                  \\,\colon\,
                  Cat
                  \\overset{Core}{\longrightarrow}
                  Grpd
                  \\overset{\tau_0}{\longrightarrow}
                  Set
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Core
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Core
                assigns the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"}> core
                ” of a category, namely the maximal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                inside it, hence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Core
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Core
                “discards” all non-invertible morphisms;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_0
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncation"} 0-truncation
                functor which turns a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-groupoid"} 1-groupoid
                into its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-groupoid"} 0-groupoid
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
          %p It may be interesting to notice here that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} Core
                is
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+2-functor"} embedding
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Grpd
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grpd \hookrightarrow Cat
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} co-reflection
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncation"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi τ
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_0
                is
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} embedding
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi Grpd
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \hookrightarrow Grpd
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflection
          %h3#DefinitionForHigherCategories For higher categories
          %p
            For any sensible notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+categories"} higher categories
            one will have corresponding analogs of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            = succeed "-operations" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncation"} truncation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PlainDecategorificationFactoredThroughCore"} (2)
          %p
            For example, for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            there are the evident notions of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core+in+a+2-category"} core in a 2-category
          %p
            More generally, for any of the models of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
              \-categories
            we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflection"} coreflection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %munderover
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Core
                          %mi n
                    %mo ↪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\infty,n) Cat
                  \\underoverset
                  {\underset{Core_n}{\longleftarrow}}
                  {\hookrightarrow}
                  {\;\; \bot \;\;}
                  (\infty,n+1)Cat
          %p
            which together with the
            = succeed "-operation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
                \-truncation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-types"}
              homotopy
              = succeed "-types" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            yields towers of higher decategorification functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Core
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi τ
                        %mi m
                  %mi m
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\infty,n+1)Cat
                  \\overset{Core_n}{\longrightarrow}
                  (\infty,n)Cat
                  \\to
                  \\cdots
                  \\to
                  (\infty,0)Cat
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\infty Grpd
                  \\overset{ \tau_m }{\longrightarrow}
                  m Grpd
                  \\to
                  \\cdots
                  \\to
                  0 Grpd
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  Set
                  \\,,
          %p any stage of which may reasonably be addressed as an intermediate stage of higher decategorification.
          %h2#ExtraStructure Extra structure
          %p
            If the category in question has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structure"} higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(C)
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
          %p
            A famous example are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+category"} fusion categories
            whose decategorifications are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verlinde+ring"}> Verlinde ring
                s
          %p
            There may also be extra structure induced more directly on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(C)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-group
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            is the decategorification of its category of bounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            and this inherits a group structure from the fact that this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} homotopy exact sequences
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The decategorifications of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
                (over any
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi FinSet
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo ∼
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      FinSet_{/\sim} \simeq \mathbb{N}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi FinDimVect
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo ∼
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      FinDimVect_{/\sim} \simeq \mathbb{N}
              %p
                For instance the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank-nullity+theorem"} rank-nullity theorem
                is the decategorification of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/splitting+lemma"} splitting lemma
                in the category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            %li
              %p
                The decategorification of the 2-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
                of (small)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
                is equivalent to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
            %li
              %p
                The decategorification in the same sense of the 2-category of (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"}> small
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
                is equivalent to the full
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+topological+spaces"} homotopy category
          %h2#parable Parable
          %p From John Baez, https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/week121.html
          %p If one studies categorification one soon discovers an amazing fact: many deep-sounding results in mathematics are just categorifications of facts we learned in high school! There is a good reason for this. All along, we have been unwittingly “decategorifying” mathematics by pretending that categories are just sets. We “decategorify” a category by forgetting about the morphisms and pretending that isomorphic objects are equal. We are left with a mere set: the set of isomorphism classes of objects.
          %p To understand this, the following parable may be useful. Long ago, when shepherds wanted to see if two herds of sheep were isomorphic, they would look for an explicit isomorphism. In other words, they would line up both herds and try to match each sheep in one herd with a sheep in the other. But one day, along came a shepherd who invented decategorification. She realized one could take each herd and “count” it, setting up an isomorphism between it and some set of “numbers”, which were nonsense words like “one, two, three,…” specially designed for this purpose. By comparing the resulting numbers, she could show that two herds were isomorphic without explicitly establishing an isomorphism! In short, by decategorifying the category of finite sets, the set of natural numbers was invented.
          %p According to this parable, decategorification started out as a stroke of mathematical genius. Only later did it become a matter of dumb habit, which we are now struggling to overcome by means of categorification. While the historical reality is far more complicated, categorification really has led to tremendous progress in mathematics during the 20th century. For example, Noether revolutionized algebraic topology by emphasizing the importance of homology groups. Previous work had focused on Betti numbers, which are just the dimensions of the rational homology groups. As with taking the cardinality of a set, taking the dimension of a vector space is a process of decategorification, since two vector spaces are isomorphic if and only if they have the same dimension. Noether noted that if we work with homology groups rather than Betti numbers, we can solve more problems, because we obtain invariants not only of spaces, but also of maps.
